WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:06.200
The Hall of Scripts had thirty doors, and no two handles alike.

2
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:11.600
From every province, messages arrived — clay, leather, bark, and silk.

3
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:18.300
One wet morning, a chapar — a courier of the relay roads — brought a tablet found beside the northern river.

4
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:21.300
One sentence. The seal had dissolved.

5
00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:27.600
The chapar did not know who had carried it, or whom it was meant to reach.

6
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:32.300
Tahmures set the tablet on a cedar stand, and asked three servants of the hall a simple question.

7
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.300
Tell me what this is.

8
00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:41.500
Tahmures expected three descriptions of the same object.

9
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:47.600
It is an old command. The hand resembles Garshasp, a captain who served your father.

10
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:50.900
It is an urgent message. I will take it to Arash, the present captain.

11
00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:55.800
The Black Div returned at dusk, soaked to the waist.

12
00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:58.200
There are eleven watchfires.

13
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:00.400
You crossed the river?

14
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:03.000
The tablet said to count them.

15
00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:05.400
I asked what the tablet was.

16
00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:07.700
The tablet asked for a count.

17
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.800
The eleven fires proved to be charcoal burners, camped outside the frontier.

18
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:19.500
Nothing written on the tablet was false — yet by midnight, three villages believed an invasion had begun.

19
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:24.600
One line had become a record, a message, and a current command — all at once.

20
00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:29.800
Tahmures first ordered Shahrasp to make the instruction longer. The scribe sharpened his reed.

21
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:36.500
Write: Examine this tablet carefully, consider every word, and tell me exactly what it means.

22
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.600
That still leaves means to carry a large burden.

23
00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:44.800
Tahmures stopped the reed. Length was not the missing piece.

24
00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:49.000
Four labels: speaker, recipient, occasion, requested result.

25
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:55.500
Three were unknown. The fourth belonged to him — and went onto a separate, sealed card.

26
00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:02.600
Now roles could be tested. 'Close the western gate' was a current task when Arash the Gatekeeper sent it to the watch —

27
00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:10.600
evidence when quoted in a trial — and mere ink under a practising apprentice's hand.

28
00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:15.800
A prompt is not one sentence. It's the complete, structured interaction an application assembles for a model:

29
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:24.000
instructions, current task, context, data, examples, constraints, output contract, interaction state.

30
00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:30.500
Before judging the answer, record what each piece of language is doing here.

31
00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:35.800
Tahmures sent a second message north, explaining the error.

32
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.800
It was shorter than his proposed revision. It named the recipient, corrected the rumour, and asked for acknowledgement.

33
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:45.400
This time, the riders came home.

34
00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:50.500
Grammar suggests a role. It doesn't establish one.
